bf63112-linux-setup
===================

This project is a thin wrapper around:

  dedbin/libfprint-bf63112
  https://github.com/dedbin/libfprint-bf63112
  commit 2e6bcf04950e02e25099f4257d042c4617cef6f6 (2026-07-22)

The BF63112 driver, the native SPI capture implementation, the vendor-engine
ABI documentation, the Wine/bubblewrap isolation design, the system installer,
the rollback logic, and the standalone verification harness are that project's
work and are licensed LGPL-2.1. This repository does not reimplement any of it.
The installer clones that repository at the pinned commit above.

What this repository adds, and claims authorship of:

  - install.sh, the single-entry wrapper that sequences the upstream tools,
    downloads the OEM matcher from Microsoft Update Catalog, verifies it against
    a pinned SHA-256, and wires PAM only with explicit consent.
  - probe-id.py, a read-only SPI register probe for the sensor ID.
  - tests/check-repo.sh, a repository gate that refuses to pass if a proprietary
    binary, a vendor package, or biometric material is present.
  - docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md, the failure catalogue observed during bring-up.

Modification of upstream files: install.sh relaxes exactly one predicate in
upstream's tools/bf63112/install-system.sh (the distro identity check), only on
systems that are not the tested Ubuntu 24.04, only at runtime, with a backup
kept beside the original. No modified copy of upstream source is redistributed
by this repository. That modification is licensed LGPL-2.1-or-later, as required
by the upstream license.

Proprietary component, NOT distributed here:

  BetterlifeEngineAdapter.dll
  size 2121392 bytes
  sha256 9f85fbdb17794d33ec021dcbcacf90733e0a055f80dd77cea6e305c96678fdeb

This is Blestech's matcher, shipped inside the OEM Windows driver. It is obtained
from Microsoft Update Catalog for hardware ID ACPI\BF63112 and remains subject
to the OEM's license. Redistribution is not permitted, which is why this project
downloads it on the user's own machine and verifies a pinned hash instead of
hosting it.

Alternative route with no proprietary code:

  https://github.com/pythontyphon/oxp3-fingerprint-linux
  https://github.com/pythontyphon/libfprint-bf63112-oxp3

Acknowledgement of the wider ecosystem: libfprint and fprintd (LGPL-2.1),
and the linux-fingerprint-drivers catalogue, which is how this sensor was
identified as a Betterlife BF63112 rather than an unsupported unknown.
